From 8320ef94ccf2ee0284910027036d4eb93e437da5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: pugant <pugant@localhost>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 18:15:53 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 17/19] vulkan: scope the tracker barrier to the involved
 buffers (A6-S1 / W6-4)

The dependency tracker's ggml_vk_sync_buffers call now emits a single
pipelineBarrier with one buffer memory barrier per involved device
buffer (the enclosing range of each dependency pair, merged per buffer)
instead of a device-wide memory barrier. Kernels touching none of the
covered ranges are no longer ordered against each other, so independent
chains keep overlapping across the barrier and cache flush/invalidation
is limited to the covered ranges.

Only the regions covered by the barrier are dropped from the tracker;
everything else stays tracked and fires its own barrier later. The
prealloc pending-read flags are cleared only when the prealloc buffer
is included at full extent, which ggml_vk_build_scoped_sync_ranges does
whenever a flag is set - preserving the exact flag-clear points the
device-wide barrier provided. Conservative fallbacks keep the
device-wide barrier when the buffer set cannot be resolved or exceeds
16 ranges, and GGML_VK_SYNC_SCOPE=0 disables scoping entirely (same
binary A/B).

Gates (zero GPU):
- tests/test-vk-sync-tracker.cpp grows a hazard oracle: the scoped
  path is simulated over randomized node groups and brute-force checked
  so every inter-group RAW/WAR/WAW pair has a barrier covering the
  overlap between the accesses - 43372 barrier ranges, 0 missed hazards;
  a sabotaged over-clearing variant fails with 236901 misses (the
  oracle has teeth).
- test-backend-ops on lavapipe (CPU Vulkan), same op set on the
  baseline, scoped and kill-switch binaries: all cases OK, identical
  sync decisions (59 = 59 = 59), no global fallback hit on the scoped
  path.

Co-Authored-By: GLM by z.ai
